# The Dockerfile does `COPY . .` in the FINAL stage, so anything not excluded here is baked into
# the published image. That is what makes this file load-bearing rather than a build-speed tweak.
#
# Dot-entries are the pointed case. A working copy accumulates local editor, tool and assistant
# state in dot-directories that are git-ignored and therefore invisible to every git-side control,
# but `COPY . .` does not consult .gitignore -- so a LOCAL build would bake that state into a
# published image layer. CI is unaffected, because it builds from a fresh checkout that has none of
# it; the exposure is the day someone builds and pushes from their own working copy.
#
# Excluded generically rather than by name, and that is deliberate: this file is COMMITTED, so an
# entry naming a specific tool would publish exactly the reference the exclusion exists to avoid.
# Nothing the image needs lives in a dot-entry -- the build uses only requirements.txt (bind-mounted
# above) and the source tree.
.*
**/.*

# VCS and CI
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github

# Local data and generated artifacts. Large, machine-specific, and regenerated at runtime.
data
models
results
logs

# Python build and cache noise
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
.venv
venv

# Tests do not run in the image
tests

# Editor and OS
.idea
.vscode
.DS_Store

# Env files: the image takes configuration from the environment, not from a baked-in file.
.env
.env.*

# Docs
README.md
LICENSE
